# Ticket lifecycle

Single source of truth for transitions must live on the backend (one table / constant). Detail responses expose `allowedTransitions`; the UI only renders what the API returns.

## Allowed transitions (exact)

- `OPEN` → `IN_PROGRESS`
- `OPEN` → `CANCELLED`
- `IN_PROGRESS` → `RESOLVED`
- `IN_PROGRESS` → `CANCELLED`
- `RESOLVED` → `CLOSED`

Wire values are uppercase `TextChoices` (`IN_PROGRESS`, not `"In Progress"`).

## Create and update

- New tickets always start as `OPEN`; client cannot set initial status
- Status changes only via dedicated transition endpoint — never via field PATCH
- PATCH of `status` → reject with a clear message pointing at the transition endpoint
- Field updates (title, description, priority, assignee) on `CLOSED` / `CANCELLED` → **409**, no changes

## Invalid transitions

- Anything outside the allow-list (including same-status, skips, reopen) → **409 Conflict**
- Status in DB must be unchanged
- Response must name current status, attempted status, and legal alternatives
- Unknown status string (not a valid enum) → **400** (malformed), not 409

## Terminal states

- `CLOSED` and `CANCELLED` are terminal — **no reopen**
- Comments are allowed on any status, including terminal (append-only; no edit/delete)

## List vs export

- List: all tickets (authenticated); support status/priority/assignee filters and `q` on title/description
- Export CSV: only `created_by = request.user`; core fields + comment count; header-only CSV if none

## Tests

- Integration tests must cover the **full** transition matrix (valid succeed, invalid rejected), not a sample
